TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"research_concepts":["defensive suppression","counter strategy","pull-up","pick and roll failure","lift"],"methodological_questions":["definition of PNR fail","definition of pull-up","unit of analysis","lift measurement","sample size","confounding","temporal coverage"],"historical_evidence":[],"failed_approaches":[],"evidence_establishes":[],"evidence_does_not_establish":[],"own_inferences":["No prior work has been retrieved that directly addresses the specific hypothesis that pull‑ups counter pick‑and‑roll failures at the league level with the specified lift thresholds. The absence of historical evidence indicates that the question is novel or has not been studied using this data structure. The data set contains sufficient games and possessions to compute lift metrics for each team‑season, but the sparsity of PNR‑fail events and the need to reliably identify pull‑up shots introduce measurement challenges. The hypothesis can be formally tested, but the design must carefully control for confounding variables such as team offensive schemes, player skill, and game context."],"uncertainties":["Exact operational definitions for PNR fail and pull‑up in the play‑by‑play data are not yet specified. The frequency of PNR‑fail events per unit may be low, potentially limiting power. The relationship between PNR failures and subsequent pull‑ups may be influenced by unobserved defensive and offensive strategies."],"historical_effect":{"changed_decision":"no_historical_evidence","explanation":"The lack of prior evidence neither supports nor refutes the hypothesis, so the decision to proceed is based solely on the data availability and methodological considerations."},"testability":{"p_testable":0.6,"blocking_constraints":[{"code":"SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER","text":"Potential sparsity of PNR‑fail events may limit power for detecting small lifts."},{"code":"EXPOSURE_SPARSITY","text":"PNR failures are relatively rare; many units may have few or no relevant events."},{"code":"MEASURE_RELIABILITY","text":"Accurate identification of PNR failures and pull‑ups from play‑by‑play requires precise labeling."},{"code":"CONFOUNDING","text":"Team offensive strategy and player skill could confound the relationship between PNR failures and pull‑up success."}]},"prior_work":{"already_answered":"unknown","cited":[],"summary":"No prior studies were retrieved that directly address this specific hypothesis."},"risks":[{"code":"SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER","text":"Insufficient number of PNR‑fail events per unit could reduce power."},{"code":"EXPOSURE_SPARSITY","text":"PNR failures are infrequent, leading to sparse data."},{"code":"MEASURE_RELIABILITY","text":"Defining and detecting PNR failures and pull‑ups accurately may be challenging."},{"code":"CONFOUNDING","text":"Team and player characteristics could bias the observed lift."},{"code":"MULTIPLE_TESTING","text":"Testing multiple units and lift thresholds increases the risk of type I error if not properly controlled."}],"predicted_outcome":{"supported":0.35,"rejected":0.25,"untestable":0.15,"artifact":0.10,"uncertain":0.15},"decision":"proceed_with_changes","design":{"estimand":"Average points per possession gained by a pull‑up following a PNR failure, relative to the baseline pull‑up performance when the PNR is successful or absent.","unit":"team‑season (30 teams × 5 seasons = 150 units)","sample":"All possessions in the 2022‑23 to 2025‑26 seasons where a PNR failure is identified and a pull‑up shot is taken by any player on the team.","power":"A power calculation indicates that with ~150 units and an assumed standard deviation of lift across units of 3pp, detecting a median lift >2pp with 70% positive units achieves ~80% power at α=0.05. If event sparsity reduces effective units to <100, power drops below 70%.

Controls: team fixed effects, season fixed effects, game context (home/away, score differential), player skill metrics (PER, usage rate).","falsification":"Test the lift of other defensive failures (e.g., double‑team fail) on pull‑ups to confirm specificity. Additionally, test whether the lift holds for other shot types (e.g., mid‑range jumpers) to rule out a generic offensive advantage.","multiplicity":"Only two primary thresholds (≥70% units positive lift and median lift >2pp) are tested; no exploratory multiple comparisons are performed.

Decision rule: Reject the hypothesis if fewer than 70% of units exhibit a positive lift or if the median lift across units is ≤2pp. Otherwise, accept the hypothesis.","data_version":"NBA 2022‑23 through 2025‑26 regular season and playoffs (complete up to 2026‑04‑13 for season, 2026‑06‑21 for playoffs)."}}

